WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.061 --> 00:00:02.728
(playful music)

00:00:04.365 --> 00:00:07.048
- I've had an absolute
fascination with sharks

00:00:07.048 --> 00:00:09.131
since I was a little kid.

00:00:12.014 --> 00:00:13.762
I knew I wanted to be a marine biologist

00:00:13.762 --> 00:00:15.388
by the time I was five,

00:00:15.388 --> 00:00:17.299
and I got to snorkel with my first shark

00:00:17.299 --> 00:00:19.170
when I was about eight years old,

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:23.920
and I was literally hooked
on these awesome animals.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.009
I'm Jillian Morris, I am
a shark conservationist,

00:00:27.009 --> 00:00:30.842
a shark diver, and an
underwater videographer.

00:00:31.967 --> 00:00:33.795
I've traveled all over the world,

00:00:33.795 --> 00:00:35.952
and I have to say that
Bimini has some of the bluest

00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:37.712
waters I've ever seen.

00:00:37.712 --> 00:00:39.946
All different shades,
some people joke about

00:00:39.946 --> 00:00:42.424
the 50 shades of blue in Bimini.

00:00:42.424 --> 00:00:44.214
The island is really, really small,

00:00:44.214 --> 00:00:45.919
it's about six miles long,

00:00:45.919 --> 00:00:48.683
but the world just below the surface

00:00:48.683 --> 00:00:50.551
is really what's incredible.

00:00:50.551 --> 00:00:52.869
That blue, crystal clear water is teeming

00:00:52.869 --> 00:00:56.160
with marine life, and is
the reason why we live

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.410
on this tiny little island.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:00.957
The great hammerhead dive site

00:01:00.957 --> 00:01:02.734
is about a quarter of a mile offshore.

00:01:02.734 --> 00:01:05.177
It's a place where in very shallow water,

00:01:05.177 --> 00:01:08.506
you're able to get in and
see these very large sharks

00:01:08.506 --> 00:01:10.339
up close and personal.

00:01:11.376 --> 00:01:14.099
It's very rare to actually
be able to be in the water

00:01:14.099 --> 00:01:16.536
with so many of them like
we can here in Bimini,

00:01:16.536 --> 00:01:20.600
especially in crystal
clear, shallow water.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:24.051
Unfortunately, sharks get
a really bad reputation.

00:01:24.051 --> 00:01:25.757
They're not man-eating monsters,

00:01:25.757 --> 00:01:28.192
they're not mindless eating machines.

00:01:28.192 --> 00:01:30.027
They're actually really intelligent,

00:01:30.027 --> 00:01:32.349
social, incredible animals.

00:01:32.349 --> 00:01:35.071
They're also really incredible
for the health of our oceans,

00:01:35.071 --> 00:01:38.969
which all of us rely on every single day.

00:01:38.969 --> 00:01:41.736
There's something really
special about hammerheads.

00:01:41.736 --> 00:01:43.890
I have been in the water
with some very big sharks,

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.123
including great whites and tiger sharks,

00:01:46.123 --> 00:01:48.684
but nothing compares to
the great hammerhead.

00:01:48.684 --> 00:01:52.423
The way they move, their
power, their grace,

00:01:52.423 --> 00:01:56.972
they can turn on a dime, and
they have those wide-set eyes.

00:01:56.972 --> 00:01:59.087
They're my favorite animal on the planet,

00:01:59.087 --> 00:02:01.767
and being able to dive
with them as much as we do,

00:02:01.767 --> 00:02:03.974
I feel really, really lucky.

00:02:03.974 --> 00:02:06.557
(gentle music)

